# Production permissions

A task points to an `authority` JSON file following
`schemas/authoring/production-authority.schema.json`. Its issuer and evidence
path/locator identify the instruction whose bytes are pinned during preparation.
The host checks real user authority: a schema authenticates no issuer and an
agent-filled form is not consent.

For a first prepared request with no registered authority, the saved work plan
exposes one shared, unapproved `initial-authority` declaration. Its task and
prepared operations are derived; issuer, instruction evidence, actors/modes and
stop conditions remain unanswered. Complete them from the actual instruction
already supplied, and choose only its permitted scope. `advance` uses the normal
authority import, which requires nonempty evidence and rejects a concurrent
registration. Registration then opens the separate exact execution judgments;
it does not send, approve a rendition or clear a stop condition. Other items of
the same plan reuse this current task authority and assess their own coverage.

Each grant names an id, actor, direct/delegated mode, allowed operations, exact
targets, protected criteria, optional request scope, submission validation modes,
and expiry/effective/revocation timestamps. Direction, edit, submit, select and
adopt are independent operations. Permission to submit is not canonical adoption
or permission to change personality. Permission to select is not permission for
another submission. Stop outside the stated scope or on ambiguous conditions.

Targets are exact strings: `purpose`, `decision:<id>`, `delivery`, `canon:sheet`,
`canon:catalog`, and changed `source:<id>`, `criterion:<id>`, `execution` or
`authority` scopes. Grant only necessary targets. Calling a repair a local edit
does not exempt its changed sources or protected criteria.

The saved [work plan](production-work-plan.md) asks once for the actual
instruction and the judgments of its exact selected use. Its curation owner
reuses eligible current grants and derives the separate select/adopt
authorizations. When several grants cover an operation, a numbered choice
appears in that operation's answer; the runtime does not choose an actor or add
a competing grant to avoid that choice. When coverage is absent, the actual
instruction must support the missing operation and target; the runtime may then
record that additional grant from the same retained instruction. A declaration
of intended use and a passing schema alone supply no permission.

One stop assessment can describe the complete requested selection and adoption
when the same actual instruction supports both. The runtime records that
judgment independently in each operation's exact authorization and checks
current scope, actor, mode and stop definitions at each effect boundary. A
changed or revoked grant returns a pending plan to current assessment; its
earlier answer cannot recreate the grant. Reassessment of an already committed
adoption claim keeps its image, original approval and destination intact.

